.Dd October 29, 2026
.Dt XFT_GPA 3
.Os xft
.Sh NAME
.Nm xft_new_gpa ,
.Nm xft_gpa_destroy ,
.Nm xft_gpa_alloc ,
.Nm xft_gpa_realloc ,
.Nm xft_gpa_free ,
.Nm xft_new_reporta ,
.Nm xft_reporta_destroy ,
.Nm xft_reporta_alloc ,
.Nm xft_reporta_realloc ,
.Nm xft_reporta_free
.Nd general-purpose and reporting allocators
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In alloc.h
.Ft t_gpa
.Fn xft_new_gpa void
.Ft void
.Fn xft_gpa_destroy "t_gpa *gpa"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_gpa_alloc "t_any alloc" "t_size size" "t_size align"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_gpa_realloc "t_any alloc" "t_buffer buf" "t_size newsize" "t_size align"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_gpa_free "t_any allocator" "t_buffer buf"
.Ft t_reporta
.Fn xft_new_reporta void
.Ft void
.Fn xft_reporta_destroy "t_reporta *gpa"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_reporta_alloc "t_any alloc" "t_size size" "t_size align"
.Ft t_buffer
.Fn xft_reporta_realloc "t_any alloc" "t_buffer buf" "t_size newsize" "t_size align"
.Ft void
.Fn xft_reporta_free "t_any allocator" "t_buffer buf"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The general-purpose allocator serves small blocks from 128 KiB slabs with
per-size free lists, and passes large blocks to the page allocator.
The reporting allocator is the same allocator with counters, and prints them
when it is destroyed.
The
.Fa alloc
and
.Fa allocator
arguments are a
.Vt t_gpa *
or a
.Vt t_reporta * ;
.Xr xft_alloc 3
describes the
.Fn xft_gpa_allocator
and
.Fn xft_reporta_allocator
vtables that wrap these functions.
.Ss Creating and destroying
.Fn xft_new_gpa
reserves the first slab of
.Dv GPA_SLABSIZE
(131072) bytes with
.Xr xft_palloc 3 .
Every slab starts with a pointer to the previous one.
.Fn xft_gpa_destroy
releases every slab.
Large blocks that were never freed are not released.
.Ss Size classes
.Fa align
must be a nonzero power of two.
A request is raised to at least 8 bytes and rounded up to a power of two, and
to the next power of two of
.Fa align
if that is larger.
The
.Dv GPA_CLASSES
(14) classes cover 8 bytes to 64 KiB.
.Pp
A larger request goes to
.Fn xft_palloc
with the larger of
.Fa size
and
.Fa align ,
and the returned
.Fa .size
is the page allocator's rounded size.
.Pp
A small request takes the head of its class's free list when
.Fa align
is at most 16 or the head is aligned to
.Fa align .
Otherwise it is carved from the current slab at the next multiple of 16, of
.Fa align
when that is larger, or of the block size for blocks under 16 bytes.
The carved range is committed with
.Fn xft_palloc_commit .
When the slab has no room, a new slab is reserved and the rest of the old one
is committed and pushed onto the free lists as power-of-two blocks, smallest
first, so that each block is aligned to its own size up to the page size.
.Pp
The returned
.Fa .size
is the rounded size, and
.Fn xft_gpa_free
and
.Fn xft_gpa_realloc
pick the class from it, so the buffer must be passed back as returned.
.Ss Freeing
.Fn xft_gpa_free
pushes a small block onto its class's free list, storing the link in its first
word, and returns a large block with
.Fn xft_palloc_free .
Slabs are not returned to the operating system before
.Fn xft_gpa_destroy ,
and freed blocks are not merged or split.
.Ss Reallocating
.Fn xft_gpa_realloc
returns
.Fa buf
unchanged when
.Fa newsize
fits in
.Fa buf Ns .size
and
.Fa buf Ns .mem
is aligned to
.Fa align .
A large block is resized with
.Fn xft_palloc_resize ,
which ignores
.Fa align .
A small block is moved: a new block is allocated, the smaller of the two sizes
is copied and
.Fa buf
is freed.
.Ss Reporting
The
.Fn xft_reporta_*
functions behave like their
.Fn xft_gpa_*
counterparts on a
.Vt t_reporta .
.Fn xft_reporta_destroy
writes a report to standard error before it releases the slabs:
.Bl -tag -width "avg frag"
.It allocs
calls to
.Fn xft_reporta_alloc ,
including the ones made by
.Fn xft_reporta_realloc
.It frees
calls to
.Fn xft_reporta_free
.It reuses
small blocks taken from a free list
.It misses
small blocks carved from a slab
.It paged
large blocks passed to the page allocator
.It slabs
slabs reserved
.It avg frag
mean bytes wasted per allocation, counting the rounding and, for carved
blocks, the alignment padding
.It leaks
allocs minus frees
.It owned
bytes in slabs, which leaves out large blocks
.El
.Pp
The report is formatted into the
.Dv REPORTA_BUFFER
(1024) byte
.Fa buffer
member, so it needs no allocation.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xft_new_gpa
and
.Fn xft_new_reporta
return an allocator whose
.Fa slab
is
.Dv nullptr
if the first slab cannot be reserved.
.Pp
.Fn xft_gpa_alloc ,
.Fn xft_gpa_realloc
and their reporting counterparts return a buffer with
.Fa .mem
set to
.Dv nullptr
and
.Fa .size
set to 0 when
.Fa align
is zero or not a power of two, or the allocation fails; a failed reallocation
leaves
.Fa buf
valid.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
t_gpa		gpa;
t_buffer	b;

gpa = xft_new_gpa();
if (gpa.slab == nullptr)
	xft_exit_process(1);
b = xft_gpa_alloc(&gpa, 100, 8);
if (b.mem != nullptr)
{
	b = xft_gpa_realloc(&gpa, b, 300, 8);
	if (b.mem != nullptr)
		xft_gpa_free(&gpa, b);
}
xft_gpa_destroy(&gpa);
.Ed
.Pp
Here the 100-byte request is served as a 128-byte block, and the reallocation
moves it to a 512-byte block.
If the reallocation fails, the 128-byte block stays allocated until
.Fn xft_gpa_destroy
releases its slab.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xft_alloc 3 ,
.Xr xft_arena 3 ,
.Xr xft_palloc 3 ,
.Xr xft 7
.Sh CAVEATS
Passing a buffer whose
.Fa .mem
is
.Dv nullptr
to the free or reallocation functions is undefined behavior.
.Pp
None of the functions lock; an allocator must not be shared between threads
without external locking.
.Vt t_ts_gpa
and
.Vt t_ts_reporta
pair one with a mutex; see
.Xr xft_alloc 3 .
.Pp
Freed blocks keep their contents except for the first word, and reused blocks
are not zeroed.
